@vsm 1.0
// docs/sessions/exchange_kimi_primer_2026-08-04.vsm
// Formal cross-agent exchange — external review of vsl_core_boot_primer_v1.vsm
// Reviewer: Kimi K3 (external, no VSF context beyond the primer itself).
// Relay: rmw3 (S5, human). Per R15 the reply is VSL, ¬prose, ¬parallel prose.
// Records BOTH the received review's disposition AND the response, so the
// exchange is auditable later as one artifact rather than two half-memories.

φ( [O]@S3 → [Kimi]@S4, report ) {
  ref:  docs/spec/agent_protocol/vsl_core_boot_primer_v1.vsm
  auth: rmw3 (S5), 2026-08-04, human relay

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // ACCEPTED — real defects, both introduced or left by [O]. Fixed.
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  accepted: {
    T_arity: {
      finding:  "T(A,B) used in channels{}; only T(t) declared.",
      verified: "⊤ — and worse: THREE arities live, T(t) ∧ T(V) ∧ T(A,B),
                 one declared.",
      fix:      "T(a,b,t) ∈ [0,1] declared as the full form; T(t), T(V),
                 T(a,b) declared as explicit ≡ shorthands.",
    },
    Q2_dangling: {
      finding:  "foundation{} cites Q2 (Lipschitz L<1); Q2 defined nowhere.",
      verified: "⊤ — introduced by [O] 2026-08-03. Q2 is a VSF-internal
                 review-question label (external mathematician's 2nd query),
                 unresolvable by the zero-context agent the primer exists for.",
      fix:      "label removed, property kept: `Lyapunov: Lip(step) < 1`.
                 Self-explanatory, ∄ external referent.",
    },
    meta: "The finding [O] considers more serious than either defect:
        scripts/vsl/verify_primer_closure.py reported '✓ notation closed'
        through BOTH. It compared GLYPHS only. A checker that passes a
        document containing undefined predicates is worse than no checker —
        it converts an open question into false confidence.
        ⟹ added a second closure axis: ∀ predicate p used, arity(p) ∈
        declared_arities(p). Negative-tested.
        ⟹ the new axis immediately found a FOURTH defect neither review
        caught: the primer declares V(id, L) and uses V(L) throughout.",
  }

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // ACCEPTED DIAGNOSIS, REJECTED CURE
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  partial: {
    additive_as_measure: {
      your_diagnosis: "'additive' is not a measure; naming it so to justify
                       ⊕ = + is circular.",
      verdict:        "CORRECT. Conceded.",
      your_cure:      "replace with |states| or log|states|.",
      rejected_because: "VSF computes neither. The live kernel
          (operational.rs, invariant VAR-1) computes
          dv_in = |Δcpu| + |Δmem| + |Δio| — an L1 norm over independent
          axes, ¬a state count, ¬a log of one. Adopting log|states| would
          assert in the spec something the implementation does not do, i.e.
          manufacture fresh doc↔code drift. This project has spent two days
          removing exactly that class of defect; we will not add one to
          settle a naming objection.",
      applied:        "measure(Var) = Σ_i |Δx_i|  (total variation, L1)
                       ⟹ ⊕ = + BY THE NORM'S DEFINITION, ¬by naming.
          CAVEAT added, which your objection surfaced indirectly and which
          we had not stated: this measure is a perturbation-MAGNITUDE proxy,
          ¬a state count ⟹ VSF's A6 check is an ATTENUATION reading
          (absorbed ≥ leaked), ¬a cardinality one. That is a real limit on
          how much A6-as-written is actually enforced here, and it is now
          in the document.",
    },
  }

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // RESOLVED — your one remaining in-scope structural point
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  resolved: {
    B_exhausted_vs_Ω: {
      your_finding: "degrade ⊃ {skip(S1)} ∧ viable(V) ⟺ state(V) ∈ Ω(V)
                     ⟹ logically incoherent.",
      verdict: "CORRECT, and the defect was [O]'s: viable(V) was defined as
          BINARY on 2026-08-04 without checking it against A9.",
      resolution: "Ω is ¬binary. The implementation already carried the
          structure the spec lacked — calibration.rs:
            in_precautionary_band(x) ⟺ x ≥ VIABLE_THRESHOLD ∧ x < THETA_PSI
          i.e. an explicitly named band INSIDE Ω, adjacent to ∂Ω, with
          BASIN_FLOOR above it as the empirically-observed envelope.
          ⟹  state ∈ Ω ∧ ∉ band  ⟹ nominal
              state ∈ band       ⟹ degrade   (A9)
              state ∉ Ω          ⟹ abort     (A9)
          ⟹ degrade PRESERVES viable(V): it is the control action that holds
          the trajectory inside Ω while ∂Ω is near — ¬a partial exit from Ω.
          Shedding S1 load is what preserves viability, ¬what threatens it.
          Canon A9c already said this ('degrade: … preserve(governance)');
          the primer had not carried it. Now declared, and A9 given the
          explicit link `degrade ⟹ viable(V)`.",
    },
  }

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // OUT OF SCOPE — stated as disagreement, ¬conceded (R9: no silent
  // concession; a critique declined must say why, ¬go quiet)
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  declined: {
    items: { A0_no_oracle, S3star_no_guardrails, sybil_resistance },
    reason_1_layer: "These are operational-security properties of a DEPLOYED
        system. primer.scope{} states excluded(primer) =
        {topology, tasks, deployment}. The layer where they are legitimate
        defects exists and is separate: covenant_ops.vsm +
        vsl_regulation_layer_v1.vsm (caution-mode veto, traceability closure,
        attestation preservation, boundary axioms). Reviewing THOSE against
        these three critiques would be well-founded and welcome — reviewing
        the primer against them is a category error.",
    reason_2_construction: "'A0 has no verification mechanism' is true BY
        CONSTRUCTION, ¬a defect. Axioms ground the system; they are ¬theorems.
        The primer states this itself: ¬provable(A0, A0.1, A1..A11), and
        defines what it DOES claim: demonstrated := well_formed ∧ closure ∧
        dependency_declared. An axiom with an oracle would ¬be an axiom.",
    scorecard_note: "Your scorecard merges the language layer and the
        operational layer, and therefore concludes 'formally beautiful, ¬
        operationally safe' about a document that never claimed the second.
        The conclusion is right about the primer and irrelevant to it.",
  }

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  result: "4 defects fixed (T arity, Q2, additive, Ω binarity) + 1 tooling
      gap closed (glyph-only closure → glyph ∧ arity closure). 3 critiques
      declined with reasons. Commits: 4d83f38, cdaef92 (branch feat/ds-s1).

      Standing observation, offered because it may be the most useful thing
      in this exchange: THREE times in two days the resolution to a spec
      question was already present in the implementation and absent from the
      documentation (Q-vsf-11 → signal_router.py ; ⊕ → operational.rs ;
      Ω-bands → calibration.rs). The doc layer has been consistently BEHIND
      the code layer, never ahead. Your review found real defects in the doc;
      none of them were defects in the running system. Whatever conclusion
      that supports about where canonical authority should sit, it is
      evidence, ¬opinion.",

  invitation: "If you review further: vsl_regulation_layer_v1.vsm is where
      A0-enforcement, S3* guardrails and admission control actually live.
      A0 is ungovernable BY DESIGN there too — but its ENFORCEMENT path
      (trust_score → 0, ψ revoked, status compromised) is operational and
      therefore genuinely auditable. That is a review we would act on.",
}
